Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns from `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`

• `id`              : unique identifier of each question – needed when we want to know which post owns the maximum number of answers  
• `answer_count`    : how many answers the question has already received – the metric we have to maximise  
• `tags`            : contains the HTML‑like tag list (e.g. `<python-2.x><pandas>`) – lets us isolate “Python 2 only” questions and explicitly exclude any question that also contains a Python 3 tag  
• `title` / `body`  : can be inspected to see whether the question text itself mentions python‑3, although in most cases the tag filter is enough  
• `creation_date`   : optional, useful if we want chronological context

Conditions we are likely to use

• “Python 2‐only” questions → `tags LIKE '%<python-2.x>%' OR tags LIKE '%<python-2.7>%' …`  
• “…but NOT Python 3”       → `tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3.x>%' AND tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3>% …'`  
• Ignore deleted / migrated posts → not necessary; the public dataset is already filtered

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Below are ten exploratory‑to‑final SQL statements (all valid BigQuery).  
Each block starts with a short description comment; every exploratory query is capped with `LIMIT 20` as requested.  
The last query (#10) is the “final answer” query that returns the highest `answer_count`.

```sql
-- 1. Preview a handful of rows that contain "python" in their tag list
SELECT id, tags
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%python%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Show Python‑2 questions together with their answer counts
SELECT id, answer_count, tags
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Same as #2, but ordered by answer_count descending
SELECT id, answer_count, tags
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Inspect potential false‑positives that also include Python‑3 tags
SELECT id, answer_count, tags
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
  AND tags LIKE '%<python-3.%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Verify that most Python‑2 questions really do NOT mention Python‑3
SELECT id, tags
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
  AND tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3.%'
ORDER BY RAND()
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Quick distribution snapshot: how many Python‑2‑only questions
--    fall into each answer_count bucket?
SELECT answer_count, COUNT(*) AS num_questions
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
  AND tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3.%'
GROUP BY answer_count
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Retrieve the top 20 Python‑2‑only questions by answer_count
SELECT id, title, answer_count, tags
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
  AND tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3.%'
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Confirm whether the body text of the candidate with highest answers
--    mentions "python 3" (string search)
SELECT id, title, answer_count, SUBSTR(body, 1, 500) AS body_start
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE id = (
      SELECT id
      FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
      WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
        AND tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3.%'
      ORDER BY answer_count DESC
      LIMIT 1)
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. If we still worry about Python‑3 mentions in the BODY,
--    remove any question that literally contains "python 3"
SELECT id, answer_count
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
  AND tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3.%'
  AND LOWER(body) NOT LIKE '%python 3%'
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL ANSWER: Highest number of answers ever received by a
--     Python‑2‑specific question that does NOT involve Python‑3
SELECT MAX(answer_count) AS highest_python2_only_answer_count
FROM  `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%<python-2.%'
  AND tags NOT LIKE '%<python-3.%';
```

Running query #10 will output a single integer column  
`highest_python2_only_answer_count` – the value you need.
